": -0.5928518176078796, "top": [[" llama", -0.5928518176078796], [" `", -0.8793145418167114]]}, {"t": ".cpp", "lp": -0.00511575723066926, "top": [[".cpp", -0.00511575723066926], [".", -6.906718730926514]]}, {"t": " because", "lp": -0.7391546368598938, "top": [[" because", -0.7391546368598938], [".", -1.0822504758834839]]}, {"t": " it", "lp": -0.01589895784854889, "top": [[" it", -0.01589895784854889], [" of", -4.188488960266113]]}, {"t": " parallel", "lp": -0.6892852187156677, "top": [[" parallel", -0.6892852187156677], [" handles", -1.2639464139938354]]}, {"t": "izes", "lp": -0.0006086293724365532, "top": [["izes", -0.0006086293724365532], ["ises", -7.959589004516602]]}, {"t": " compilation", "lp": -0.22375094890594482, "top": [[" compilation", -0.22375094890594482], [" better", -3.0613439083099365]]}, {"t": " more", "lp": -0.381343811750412, "top": [[" more", -0.381343811750412], [" better", -2.69195556640625]]}, {"t": " aggressively", "lp": -0.284060001373291, "top": [[" aggressively", -0.284060001373291], [" efficiently", -1.6428436040878296]]}], "text": "Here is a step-by-step breakdown of the `build()` function in your PKGBUILD.\n\n### 1. Environment Setup\n```bash\nsource /opt/intel/oneapi/setvars.sh --force\n```\n*   **Purpose:** This script sets up the environment variables required for Intel OneAPI tools (compilers, libraries, paths).\n*   **`--force`:** This flag forces the script to overwrite existing environment variables. This is crucial in Arch Linux packaging because `makepkg` might have set its own compiler flags or paths that could conflict with Intel's specific requirements. Without `--force`, the Intel compiler might not find its libraries or might use the wrong paths.\n\n### 2. Error Handling\n```bash\nset -e\n```\n*   **Purpose:** This is a standard Bash safety measure. It tells the shell to **exit immediately** if any command returns a non-zero exit code (an error).\n*   **Why it's here:** If the Intel environment setup fails, or if CMake fails, the build stops immediately rather than continuing with broken state and producing a corrupted package.\n\n### 3. CMake Configuration\n```bash\ncmake -S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\" -B \"${srcdir}/build\" -G Ninja \\\n```\n*   **`-S \"${srcdir}/llama.cpp\"`:** Specifies the **source directory**. This is where the downloaded git repository lives.\n*   **`-B \"${srcdir}/build\"`:** Specifies the **build directory**. CMake will generate all intermediate files (object files, makefiles) here, keeping the source tree clean.\n*   **`-G Ninja`:** Sets the build system generator to **Ninja**. Ninja is significantly faster than Make for large C++ projects like llama.cpp because it parallelizes compilation more aggressively"}